WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.118 --> 00:00:02.569
- [Mark] There's the rulers.

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.147
(low chime)

00:00:04.147 --> 00:00:04.980
The reverb tank.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.849
(metal clanking)

00:00:06.849 --> 00:00:08.097
The ebow.

00:00:08.097 --> 00:00:11.930
(ominous, high-pitched whine)

00:00:13.638 --> 00:00:14.796
- [Tony] A normal instrument.

00:00:14.796 --> 00:00:17.297
You're playing it and you're
expecting it to have a sound

00:00:17.297 --> 00:00:18.714
that is pleasing.

00:00:21.057 --> 00:00:22.076
But with an instrument like this,

00:00:22.076 --> 00:00:24.996
the goal is to just produce sounds that,

00:00:24.996 --> 00:00:27.413
in this case, are disturbing.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.693
(nightmare machine blares)

00:00:39.836 --> 00:00:43.025
(low strings music)

00:00:43.025 --> 00:00:45.177
- [Mark] The Apprehension
Engine was an instrument

00:00:45.177 --> 00:00:48.348
that creates a variety of really creepy

00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:51.431
and some would say horrifying sounds.

00:00:55.657 --> 00:00:57.087
My name is Mark Korven.

00:00:57.087 --> 00:00:58.753
I'm a film music composer and lately,

00:00:58.753 --> 00:01:01.396
I've been focusing on horror movie scores.

00:01:01.396 --> 00:01:04.428
(ominous strings)

00:01:04.428 --> 00:01:06.937
Two of my biggest hits as far
as horror films are concerned

00:01:06.937 --> 00:01:08.156
was from the late nineties,

00:01:08.156 --> 00:01:09.739
a film called Cube.

00:01:11.588 --> 00:01:14.260
And, most recently, The Witch.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.927
(ominous music)

00:01:18.271 --> 00:01:20.438
(tapping)

00:01:22.039 --> 00:01:24.098
I originally commissioned
the Apprehension Engine

00:01:24.098 --> 00:01:27.871
because I was tired of
the same digital samples,

00:01:27.871 --> 00:01:30.700
which resulted in a lot of sameness

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.159
of a lot of horror musics.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:35.540
(ominous music)

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:38.569
So, I was looking for something
more experimental,

00:01:38.569 --> 00:01:41.848
more acoustic that would
give me a little bit more

00:01:41.848 --> 00:01:43.598
of an original sound.

00:01:44.919 --> 00:01:49.086
That's where I contacted my
friend, Tony Duggan-Smith.

00:01:52.259 --> 00:01:55.201
- So, Mark called me asking me
to create a crazy instrument

00:01:55.201 --> 00:01:56.618
for horror films.

00:01:57.671 --> 00:01:59.591
Because I'd never done
anything like that before,

00:01:59.591 --> 00:02:02.039
it made me empty out all my
pockets and all my drawers

00:02:02.039 --> 00:02:04.011
of any knick-knacks and bits of bobs

00:02:04.011 --> 00:02:07.028
I could possibly string together
to make it happen for him.

00:02:07.028 --> 00:02:08.631
And this is what came of it.

00:02:08.631 --> 00:02:11.881
(off-key string music)

00:02:13.191 --> 00:02:15.201
You're dealing with things
that stir primal emotions

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.284
and feelings.

00:02:17.919 --> 00:02:20.250
And there actually is a skill
set that you have to acquire

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.121
in order to get the most out of it.

00:02:22.121 --> 00:02:25.371
(off-key string music)

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:29.029
- [Mark] It expresses what
really can't be expressed

00:02:29.029 --> 00:02:30.196
any other way.

00:02:31.881 --> 00:02:34.772
It's not music in the
traditional sense, at all.

00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:36.271
But, let me put this way.

00:02:36.271 --> 00:02:40.471
The Apprehension Engine
definitely evokes an emotion,

00:02:40.471 --> 00:02:42.532
so I would call it music.

00:02:42.532 --> 00:02:45.199
(tapping)

00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:47.532
(grinding)

